📚 Master Basic Data Structures (Full Clear Explanation)

1. 📦 Arrays

➔ Definition:

Array is a fixed-size collection of elements of the same type, stored contiguously in memory.

Each element can be accessed directly by its index (starting from 0).

➔ Properties:
Size is fixed at the time of creation.

Fast access: O(1) time to access any element.

Insertion/deletion is costly (you might have to shift elements).

➔ Example (Java):
java

int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
System.out.println(arr[2]); // Output: 3

➔ Real-Time Example:
Student marks stored in an array.

Leaderboard scores stored as arrays.

Colors in an image stored as pixel arrays.

2. 📄 Strings
➔ Definition:
String is a sequence of characters.

In many languages like Java, a String is internally an array of characters.

➔ Properties:
Immutable in languages like Java (once created, cannot be changed).

Can be concatenated, compared, sliced, etc.

➔ Example (Java):
java


String s = "Hello World";
System.out.println(s.charAt(1)); // Output: e

➔ Real-Time Example:
Usernames, Passwords are stored as strings.

Chat messages in WhatsApp are strings.

URLs in browsers are strings.

3. 🔗 Linked List (Singly)
➔ Definition:
Singly Linked List is a sequence of nodes where each node points to the next node.

It does not point backward.

➔ Properties:
Dynamic size.

Insertion and deletion are faster (O(1)) compared to arrays for certain cases (no shifting).

No direct access (must traverse to find a node).

➔ Node Structure:
java

class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;
}
➔ Example:
java

Node head = new Node();
head.data = 10;
head.next = new Node();
head.next.data = 20;

➔ Real-Time Example:
Browser history (forward and backward traversal).

Music playlist (next song).

Low-memory embedded devices where you want efficient insertion/deletion.

4. 🔗🔙 Linked List (Doubly)
➔ Definition:
Doubly Linked List is a sequence of nodes where each node points to both the next and the previous node.

➔ Properties:
Can traverse both forward and backward.

Requires extra space for the extra pointer (prev).

➔ Node Structure:
java

class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;
    Node prev;
}
➔ Example:
java

Node head = new Node();
head.data = 10;
head.next = new Node();
head.next.data = 20;
head.next.prev = head;
➔ Real-Time Example:
Undo and Redo in software (Word, Photoshop).

Music player next/previous song navigation.

Doubly linked tabs in browsers.

5. 🥞 Stacks
➔ Definition:
Stack is a linear data structure that follows Last In, First Out (LIFO) order.

The last element inserted is the first one to be removed.

➔ Properties:
Operations: push (insert), pop (remove), peek (view top element).

Used in recursion, backtracking, etc.

➔ Example (Java using Stack class):
java

import java.util.Stack;
Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
System.out.println(stack.pop()); // Output: 20
➔ Real-Time Example:
Undo operations (word editors).

Back button in browsers.

Syntax parsing (compilers check {}, (), []).

6. 🚎 Queues
➔ Definition:
Queue is a linear data structure that follows First In, First Out (FIFO) order.

The first element inserted is the first one to be removed.

➔ Properties:
Operations: enqueue (insert at rear), dequeue (remove from front).

➔ Example (Java using Queue interface):

Edit
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.add(10);
queue.add(20);
System.out.println(queue.remove()); // Output: 10
➔ Real-Time Example:
Printer queue.

Customer service queues.

CPU process scheduling.

